          "content": "A civil engineer rarely receives a load as a single force with a neat arrow. Occupancy, masonry, finishes, earth pressure and wind act over lengths, areas or volumes. The first decision is the boundary of the isolated system and the geometric conversion from actual load to an equivalent resultant. Unit integrity is essential: kN/m on a beam is not interchangeable with kN/m\u00b2 on a slab. A structural strip width and tributary geometry are needed to transform area loading into a line load. The resultant of distributed loading reproduces force and moment for global equilibrium, but replacing a distributed load with one point force is generally unsuitable for calculating the internal shear diagram.\n\n**Engineering boundary:** These exercises analyze idealized statics models for education. They do **not** certify structural strength, earthquake/wind resistance, geotechnical bearing, connection design, construction safety, or compliance with any jurisdiction\u2019s building code. Real works require local standards, authoritative loading data and qualified professional checking."
