          "id": "sec-1-6",
          "secNumber": "1.6",
          "heading": "Resultants, line of action and load auditing",
          "content": "## Lesson 1.4: Resultants, line of action and load auditing\n\nTo compare two loading systems, check both their net force and moment about a common reference point. For mixed loading use the centroid of the load-intensity diagram. Load ledgers preserve the assumptions and allow other reviewers to reproduce the result.\n\n\n### Governing relationships\n\nFor parallel forces Wi at distances xi, W=\u03a3Wi and xbar=\u03a3(Wi xi)/\u03a3Wi when W is nonzero. A zero force resultant may still leave a nonzero couple.\n\n\n### Worked engineering example\n\nA 10 kN load at x=1 m and 20 kN at x=4 m give W=30 kN and xbar=(10+80)/30=3 m. The load is not located at the arithmetic average x=2.5 m.\n\n\n### Model audit and common error\n\nKeep a separate record of action source, sign, unit, tributary dimension, position and whether the load is simultaneous.\n\n\n### Interpretation task\n\nPrepare a free-body sketch of a civil infrastructure element involving resultants, line of action and load auditing. Mark coordinate axes, every restraint action, all known magnitudes and signed reference distances. Explain which connections or soil assumptions would invalidate the model. Report results in both a compact table and an independent equilibrium check.\n\n\n### Engineering discussion\n\nThis topic should be evaluated with the full load path rather than by its equation alone. For forces, moments and structural load representation, the quantities that matter are the action itself, where it enters the idealized body, and which physical connection or foundation provides the balancing action. A model that omits one of these produces a formally neat answer without identifying the actual mechanism. Separating gross structural equilibrium from local member strength prevents that confusion. The analyst should first decide what the isolated body is, then measure or state geometry, then apply equilibrium. If an intermediate step changes the sign convention or datum, it should be flagged in the calculation record. Numerical solutions should preserve enough precision for checks but not imply more site accuracy than the input geometry supports."
