          "secNumber": "3.3",
          "heading": "Two-force members and truss geometry",
          "content": "## Lesson 3.1: Two-force members and truss geometry\n\nA force at each end of an isolated pin-connected member must be collinear with the member when no other load or couple acts on the member. This result justifies labeling each ideal truss member with one unknown axial magnitude.\n\n\n### Governing relationships\n\nPlanar joint equilibrium gives \u03a3Fx=0 and \u03a3Fy=0 at each joint. Member directions follow measured joint coordinates. Tension is conventionally positive away from the joint.\n\n\n### Worked engineering example\n\nAt a symmetric roof-truss apex with vertical load 30 kN and two equal diagonals at 45\u00b0 above horizontal, each diagonal must deliver 30/(2sin45\u00b0)=21.21 kN vertical-supporting compression magnitude.\n\n\n### Model audit and common error\n\nIf a panel is loaded between joints, the ideal two-force model may be invalid for that member.\n\n\n### Interpretation task\n\nPrepare a free-body sketch of a civil infrastructure element involving two-force members and truss geometry. Mark coordinate axes, every restraint action, all known magnitudes and signed reference distances. Explain which connections or soil assumptions would invalidate the model. Report results in both a compact table and an independent equilibrium check.\n\n\n### Engineering discussion\n\nThis topic should be evaluated with the full load path rather than by its equation alone. For trusses, joints, sections and idealization, the quantities that matter are the action itself, where it enters the idealized body, and which physical connection or foundation provides the balancing action. A model that omits one of these produces a formally neat answer without identifying the actual mechanism. Separating gross structural equilibrium from local member strength prevents that confusion. The analyst should first decide what the isolated body is, then measure or state geometry, then apply equilibrium. If an intermediate step changes the sign convention or datum, it should be flagged in the calculation record. Numerical solutions should preserve enough precision for checks but not imply more site accuracy than the input geometry supports."
