          "content": "## Lesson 3.4: Planar determinacy and member stability\n\nFor a simple planar pin-jointed truss, the count m+r=2j is a necessary but not sufficient condition for static determinacy; geometry can still be unstable. Compression-member buckling must be evaluated separately using member stiffness and end restraint.\n\n\n### Governing relationships\n\nHere m is number of truss members, r support-reaction components and j joints. A simple determinate truss typically has m=2j\u22123 with three independent external reaction components.\n\n\n### Worked engineering example\n\nA four-joint rectangle without a diagonal has four perimeter members, so m=4 while 2j\u22123=5. With ordinary pin joints the assembly is a mechanism until stabilized by a suitable diagonal or other restraint.\n\n\n### Model audit and common error\n\nA high member force is not evidence of safe capacity; this chapter solves demand only.\n\n\n### Interpretation task\n\nPrepare a free-body sketch of a civil infrastructure element involving planar determinacy and member stability. Mark coordinate axes, every restraint action, all known magnitudes and signed reference distances. Explain which connections or soil assumptions would invalidate the model. Report results in both a compact table and an independent equilibrium check.\n\n\n### Engineering discussion\n\nThis topic should be evaluated with the full load path rather than by its equation alone. For trusses, joints, sections and idealization, the quantities that matter are the action itself, where it enters the idealized body, and which physical connection or foundation provides the balancing action. A model that omits one of these produces a formally neat answer without identifying the actual mechanism. Separating gross structural equilibrium from local member strength prevents that confusion. The analyst should first decide what the isolated body is, then measure or state geometry, then apply equilibrium. If an intermediate step changes the sign convention or datum, it should be flagged in the calculation record. Numerical solutions should preserve enough precision for checks but not imply more site accuracy than the input geometry supports."
