          "heading": "Flexible cables and sag sensitivity",
          "content": "## Lesson 4.2: Flexible cables and sag sensitivity\n\nAn ideal cable carries axial tension only, and the cable shape depends on load distribution. For uniform load per horizontal projection, an ideal parabolic shape is appropriate; a cable under its own constant weight per actual cable length has a catenary shape.\n\n\n### Governing relationships\n\nFor horizontal span L, midspan sag f and uniform vertical load w per unit horizontal span, the parabolic model gives H=wL\u00b2/(8f). Approximate end tension is sqrt(H\u00b2+(wL/2)\u00b2).\n\n\n### Worked engineering example\n\nFor L=20 m, sag f=2 m, and w=1 kN/m horizontal, H=25 kN and support vertical reaction is 10 kN; end tension is sqrt(25\u00b2+10\u00b2)=26.93 kN.\n\n\n### Model audit and common error\n\nSmall sag increases horizontal force: a visually shallow cable is not necessarily a lightly loaded cable.\n\n\n### Interpretation task\n\nPrepare a free-body sketch of a civil infrastructure element involving flexible cables and sag sensitivity. Mark coordinate axes, every restraint action, all known magnitudes and signed reference distances. Explain which connections or soil assumptions would invalidate the model. Report results in both a compact table and an independent equilibrium check.\n\n\n### Engineering discussion\n\nThis topic should be evaluated with the full load path rather than by its equation alone. For frames, cables, arches and structural load paths, the quantities that matter are the action itself, where it enters the idealized body, and which physical connection or foundation provides the balancing action. A model that omits one of these produces a formally neat answer without identifying the actual mechanism. Separating gross structural equilibrium from local member strength prevents that confusion. The analyst should first decide what the isolated body is, then measure or state geometry, then apply equilibrium. If an intermediate step changes the sign convention or datum, it should be flagged in the calculation record. Numerical solutions should preserve enough precision for checks but not imply more site accuracy than the input geometry supports."
