          "content": "## Lesson 4.3: Arches, horizontal thrust and load line\n\nArches redirect loading partly through compression and may generate significant horizontal support thrust. A funicular arch under its design loading may carry little bending, but other loading patterns and support movements introduce bending.\n\n\n### Governing relationships\n\nFor an ideal three-hinged parabolic arch with span L, central rise f and uniform vertical load w, horizontal thrust H=wL\u00b2/(8f), obtained from zero bending moment at the crown hinge.\n\n\n### Worked engineering example\n\nA symmetric 30 m arch with rise 5 m and w=3 kN/m has H=3\u00d7900/(40)=67.5 kN. Global vertical support reaction is wL/2=45 kN at each end.\n\n\n### Model audit and common error\n\nAn arch solution is valid only with its specified hinge, geometry and loading assumptions.\n\n\n### Interpretation task\n\nPrepare a free-body sketch of a civil infrastructure element involving arches, horizontal thrust and load line. Mark coordinate axes, every restraint action, all known magnitudes and signed reference distances. Explain which connections or soil assumptions would invalidate the model. Report results in both a compact table and an independent equilibrium check.\n\n\n### Engineering discussion\n\nThis topic should be evaluated with the full load path rather than by its equation alone. For frames, cables, arches and structural load paths, the quantities that matter are the action itself, where it enters the idealized body, and which physical connection or foundation provides the balancing action. A model that omits one of these produces a formally neat answer without identifying the actual mechanism. Separating gross structural equilibrium from local member strength prevents that confusion. The analyst should first decide what the isolated body is, then measure or state geometry, then apply equilibrium. If an intermediate step changes the sign convention or datum, it should be flagged in the calculation record. Numerical solutions should preserve enough precision for checks but not imply more site accuracy than the input geometry supports."
