          "content": "## Lesson 4.4: Load-path continuity and construction detailing\n\nA system can satisfy an attractive free-body calculation and still be unbuildable because forces cannot cross an absent or weak connection. A useful load-path diagram traces each force to ground and identifies the member or interface responsible for every transfer.\n\n\n### Governing relationships\n\nAt a floor-to-beam-to-column interface, the tributary floor load enters the beam, beam end reactions load columns and columns send resultant forces into the foundation. Compatibility and deformation redistribute load when the system is indeterminate.\n\n\n### Worked engineering example\n\nA precast plank bearing on a girder transfers vertical reaction through contact. If the bearing seat is removed during construction, the intended gravity load path does not exist even though a drawing still shows nominal support reactions.\n\n\n### Model audit and common error\n\nEquilibrium of individual elements must agree with the complete structural assembly.\n\n\n### Interpretation task\n\nPrepare a free-body sketch of a civil infrastructure element involving load-path continuity and construction detailing. Mark coordinate axes, every restraint action, all known magnitudes and signed reference distances. Explain which connections or soil assumptions would invalidate the model. Report results in both a compact table and an independent equilibrium check.\n\n\n### Engineering discussion\n\nThis topic should be evaluated with the full load path rather than by its equation alone. For frames, cables, arches and structural load paths, the quantities that matter are the action itself, where it enters the idealized body, and which physical connection or foundation provides the balancing action. A model that omits one of these produces a formally neat answer without identifying the actual mechanism. Separating gross structural equilibrium from local member strength prevents that confusion. The analyst should first decide what the isolated body is, then measure or state geometry, then apply equilibrium. If an intermediate step changes the sign convention or datum, it should be flagged in the calculation record. Numerical solutions should preserve enough precision for checks but not imply more site accuracy than the input geometry supports."
