          "heading": "Structural section bookkeeping and unit conversions",
          "content": "## Lesson 5.4: Structural section bookkeeping and unit conversions\n\nA change of geometric scale affects area as length squared and area second moment as length to the fourth power. Keeping explicit units is crucial when transferring calculations between architectural millimetres and analysis metres.\n\n\n### Governing relationships\n\n1 m\u2074 =10\u00b9\u00b2 mm\u2074; 1 cm\u2074=10\u2074 mm\u2074. For a composite section, Ixx=\u03a3(Ixx,i,c+Ai(yi\u2212ybar)\u00b2).\n\n\n### Worked engineering example\n\nA steel plate 10 mm thick and 100 mm wide has Ixx=100\u00d710\u00b3/12=8333.3 mm\u2074 about its own horizontal centroidal axis. Placing it 100 mm away from a composite centroid adds 1000\u00d7100\u00b2=10 million mm\u2074.\n\n\n### Model audit and common error\n\nNever add an area in mm\u00b2 directly to a second moment in mm\u2074.\n\n\n### Interpretation task\n\nPrepare a free-body sketch of a civil infrastructure element involving structural section bookkeeping and unit conversions. Mark coordinate axes, every restraint action, all known magnitudes and signed reference distances. Explain which connections or soil assumptions would invalidate the model. Report results in both a compact table and an independent equilibrium check.\n\n\n### Engineering discussion\n\nThis topic should be evaluated with the full load path rather than by its equation alone. For centroids, second moments and composite cross sections, the quantities that matter are the action itself, where it enters the idealized body, and which physical connection or foundation provides the balancing action. A model that omits one of these produces a formally neat answer without identifying the actual mechanism. Separating gross structural equilibrium from local member strength prevents that confusion. The analyst should first decide what the isolated body is, then measure or state geometry, then apply equilibrium. If an intermediate step changes the sign convention or datum, it should be flagged in the calculation record. Numerical solutions should preserve enough precision for checks but not imply more site accuracy than the input geometry supports."
