          "id": "sec-6-3",
          "secNumber": "6.3",
          "heading": "Coulomb friction with unilateral contact",
          "content": "## Lesson 6.1: Coulomb friction with unilateral contact\n\nA dry contact model bounds tangential friction by \u03bcN. In static equilibrium the friction magnitude adjusts to demand up to its upper limit. A compressive normal reaction N cannot become negative when contact cannot carry tension.\n\n\n### Governing relationships\n\nFor a simple horizontal base without other vertical loads, N=W and static friction may satisfy |F|\u2264\u03bcsN. At impending slip, |F|=\u03bcsN under the idealized model.\n\n\n### Worked engineering example\n\nA 60 kN gravity load on a level base with coefficient \u03bc=0.4 offers at most 24 kN nominal friction resistance. A 10 kN horizontal demand requires 10 kN static friction, not automatically 24 kN.\n\n\n### Model audit and common error\n\nFriction coefficient values depend on interface condition, moisture, contamination and testing; do not treat classroom numbers as design guarantees.\n\n\n### Interpretation task\n\nPrepare a free-body sketch of a civil infrastructure element involving coulomb friction with unilateral contact. Mark coordinate axes, every restraint action, all known magnitudes and signed reference distances. Explain which connections or soil assumptions would invalidate the model. Report results in both a compact table and an independent equilibrium check.\n\n\n### Engineering discussion\n\nThis topic should be evaluated with the full load path rather than by its equation alone. For friction, sliding, overturning and stability, the quantities that matter are the action itself, where it enters the idealized body, and which physical connection or foundation provides the balancing action. A model that omits one of these produces a formally neat answer without identifying the actual mechanism. Separating gross structural equilibrium from local member strength prevents that confusion. The analyst should first decide what the isolated body is, then measure or state geometry, then apply equilibrium. If an intermediate step changes the sign convention or datum, it should be flagged in the calculation record. Numerical solutions should preserve enough precision for checks but not imply more site accuracy than the input geometry supports."
