          "content": "## Lesson 6.3: Overturning moments and contact resultant\n\nOverturning resistance is a moment balance around a specified toe. A nominal positive stabilizing moment does not alone ensure compression across the entire base; resultant eccentricity can create partial contact.\n\n\n### Governing relationships\n\nFor base width B, downward load W centered at B/2 and lateral force H at height h, stabilizing moment about the toe is WB/2, overturning moment is Hh. Resultant eccentricity e=M/W in a simple vertical-resultant model.\n\n\n### Worked engineering example\n\nFor B=2 m, W=100 kN, H=20 kN at h=3 m, resisting moment WB/2=100 kN\u00b7m and overturning moment Hh=60 kN\u00b7m. Nominal toe ratio is 1.67.\n\n\n### Model audit and common error\n\nLocal bearing pressures, soil capacity and load factors are beyond the friction-and-moment calculation.\n\n\n### Interpretation task\n\nPrepare a free-body sketch of a civil infrastructure element involving overturning moments and contact resultant. Mark coordinate axes, every restraint action, all known magnitudes and signed reference distances. Explain which connections or soil assumptions would invalidate the model. Report results in both a compact table and an independent equilibrium check.\n\n\n### Engineering discussion\n\nThis topic should be evaluated with the full load path rather than by its equation alone. For friction, sliding, overturning and stability, the quantities that matter are the action itself, where it enters the idealized body, and which physical connection or foundation provides the balancing action. A model that omits one of these produces a formally neat answer without identifying the actual mechanism. Separating gross structural equilibrium from local member strength prevents that confusion. The analyst should first decide what the isolated body is, then measure or state geometry, then apply equilibrium. If an intermediate step changes the sign convention or datum, it should be flagged in the calculation record. Numerical solutions should preserve enough precision for checks but not imply more site accuracy than the input geometry supports."
