          "content": "## Lesson 7.2: Lever, pulley and linkage equilibrium\n\nMechanical advantage is not a free multiplication of energy. A large displacement at a low-force point balances a smaller displacement at a high-force point in an ideal lossless model.\n\n\n### Governing relationships\n\nFor an ideal pivoted lever at equilibrium, F1a=F2b from moments or F1\u03b4x1=F2\u03b4x2 from virtual work.\n\n\n### Worked engineering example\n\nAn ideal lever with a=1.2 m, b=0.3 m and a 400 N resisting load needs F=400\u00d70.3/1.2=100 N. The effort point moves four times farther.\n\n\n### Model audit and common error\n\nBearing friction, lever bending and out-of-plane eccentricity can invalidate the ideal result.\n\n\n### Interpretation task\n\nPrepare a free-body sketch of a civil infrastructure element involving lever, pulley and linkage equilibrium. Mark coordinate axes, every restraint action, all known magnitudes and signed reference distances. Explain which connections or soil assumptions would invalidate the model. Report results in both a compact table and an independent equilibrium check.\n\n\n### Engineering discussion\n\nThis topic should be evaluated with the full load path rather than by its equation alone. For virtual work, equilibrium checks and energy ideas, the quantities that matter are the action itself, where it enters the idealized body, and which physical connection or foundation provides the balancing action. A model that omits one of these produces a formally neat answer without identifying the actual mechanism. Separating gross structural equilibrium from local member strength prevents that confusion. The analyst should first decide what the isolated body is, then measure or state geometry, then apply equilibrium. If an intermediate step changes the sign convention or datum, it should be flagged in the calculation record. Numerical solutions should preserve enough precision for checks but not imply more site accuracy than the input geometry supports."
