          "secNumber": "7.5",
          "heading": "Energy and compatibility introduction",
          "content": "## Lesson 7.3: Energy and compatibility introduction\n\nRigid equilibrium sets net force and moment to zero, whereas deformable structural analysis also needs displacement compatibility and material behavior. The energy viewpoint is valuable precisely because it connects internal deformation and external work.\n\n\n### Governing relationships\n\nFor a linearly elastic spring, U=\u00bdk\u03b4\u00b2 and force F=k\u03b4. The derivative dU/d\u03b4=k\u03b4 recovers elastic force in a one-degree-of-freedom system.\n\n\n### Worked engineering example\n\nA linear spring with k=5000 N/m stretched \u03b4=0.02 m stores U=0.5\u00d75000\u00d70.02\u00b2=1 J and produces F=100 N.\n\n\n### Model audit and common error\n\nA static force calculation does not prove that the system stays in the small-deformation regime.\n\n\n### Interpretation task\n\nPrepare a free-body sketch of a civil infrastructure element involving energy and compatibility introduction. Mark coordinate axes, every restraint action, all known magnitudes and signed reference distances. Explain which connections or soil assumptions would invalidate the model. Report results in both a compact table and an independent equilibrium check.\n\n\n### Engineering discussion\n\nThis topic should be evaluated with the full load path rather than by its equation alone. For virtual work, equilibrium checks and energy ideas, the quantities that matter are the action itself, where it enters the idealized body, and which physical connection or foundation provides the balancing action. A model that omits one of these produces a formally neat answer without identifying the actual mechanism. Separating gross structural equilibrium from local member strength prevents that confusion. The analyst should first decide what the isolated body is, then measure or state geometry, then apply equilibrium. If an intermediate step changes the sign convention or datum, it should be flagged in the calculation record. Numerical solutions should preserve enough precision for checks but not imply more site accuracy than the input geometry supports."
