          "heading": "Theoretical Framework: Civil Loading Cases, Construction Stages and Modeling Uncertainty",
          "content": "A structural response depends not only on the magnitude of a load but also on when and where it is applied. A partially constructed structure may have different boundary conditions from its finished condition. Formwork, shoring and temporary bracing can redirect load paths; removing them prematurely can invalidate final-stage calculations. Students should learn to represent each stage with its own free-body diagram and to document assumptions rather than mixing permanent and temporary conditions. Actual design must follow adopted building codes and professionally determined load combinations; introductory case studies intentionally avoid declaring code compliance.\n\n**Engineering boundary:** These exercises analyze idealized statics models for education. They do **not** certify structural strength, earthquake/wind resistance, geotechnical bearing, connection design, construction safety, or compliance with any jurisdiction\u2019s building code. Real works require local standards, authoritative loading data and qualified professional checking."
