          "secNumber": "8.6",
          "heading": "Sensitivity, documentation and the design boundary",
          "content": "## Lesson 8.4: Sensitivity, documentation and the design boundary\n\nAn engineering hand calculation is auditable when geometry, loads, assumptions, units and uncertainty are visible. Sensitivity studies test how results shift when important unknowns vary. This is not the same as selecting legal design loads or conducting final safety assessment.\n\n\n### Governing relationships\n\nFor RB=Pa/L from a point load P at a, \u2202RB/\u2202a=P/L. The right reaction increases linearly as the load moves right.\n\n\n### Worked engineering example\n\nFor P=18 kN, L=9 m, moving the load 0.2 m right increases RB by 18\u00d70.2/9=0.4 kN. A survey error of that size matters when the result lies close to a threshold.\n\n\n### Model audit and common error\n\nNever describe an educational equilibrium calculation as structural design approval.\n\n\n### Interpretation task\n\nPrepare a free-body sketch of a civil infrastructure element involving sensitivity, documentation and the design boundary. Mark coordinate axes, every restraint action, all known magnitudes and signed reference distances. Explain which connections or soil assumptions would invalidate the model. Report results in both a compact table and an independent equilibrium check.\n\n\n### Engineering discussion\n\nThis topic should be evaluated with the full load path rather than by its equation alone. For civil loading cases, construction stages and modeling uncertainty, the quantities that matter are the action itself, where it enters the idealized body, and which physical connection or foundation provides the balancing action. A model that omits one of these produces a formally neat answer without identifying the actual mechanism. Separating gross structural equilibrium from local member strength prevents that confusion. The analyst should first decide what the isolated body is, then measure or state geometry, then apply equilibrium. If an intermediate step changes the sign convention or datum, it should be flagged in the calculation record. Numerical solutions should preserve enough precision for checks but not imply more site accuracy than the input geometry supports."
